// vygenerováno scripts/gen-weekly.mjs, needitovat ručně
export const WEEKLY: { subject: string; preheader: string; body: string }[] = [
  { subject: "How to order when you eat out", preheader: "Protein first, then vegetables, sauce on the side.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nThe menu is open and the waiter is already looking at you.\n\nBuild the plate the same way as at home. About a quarter is protein: meat, fish, eggs, beans or lentils. About a quarter is a quality carb: potatoes, rice, or a wholegrain side. About half is vegetables. Fat is seasoning: a little oil or sauce. Drink water, unsweetened tea, or coffee without the sweet extras.\n\nRead the menu in that order. Protein first. Vegetables next. One carb portion. Sauce on the side. Fat packs a lot of calories into a small volume, and pouring by eye adds them fast.\n\nTotal energy over the day and the week is what decides fat loss. Fit the meal you want into that total. Sweet drinks, juice and alcohol add energy, and you don't feel them as fullness the way you feel food. Alcohol often leads to more food on top.\n\nSweets can stay too. Fit them into the day. A lifelong ban is hard to keep, and people often quit.\n\nThe plate, in full: {{link_article:what-to-eat-to-lose-fat}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Portions when there's no scale around", preheader: "Fourteen honest days. Then only the foods that fool the eye.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nYou don't have to weigh every meal for the rest of the year.\n\nCounting is there to train your eye. For fourteen days, log everything. Cooking fat. Drinks. Bites while you cook. The things you tell yourself don't count. Very often there was no deficit at all, because those bits were missing from the log.\n\nAfter two to three months, most clients estimate the normal meals well enough. They go back to the scale only for the foods they keep getting wrong. Those are usually sides, nuts and sauces. A piece of meat or fish is easier to see. Oil, nuts and the sauce on top are where the eye goes low.\n\nThere's a classic study on this blind spot. People said they ate under 1,200 kcal a day and still didn't lose weight. Measured energy use was within 5% of the predicted number, so their metabolism wasn't the problem. They underreported intake by 47% on average, and they overreported activity by 51%. Nobody was lying on purpose. They estimated.\n\nSo the scale is a teacher for fourteen days. After that, weigh the foods that still fool you, and estimate the rest.\n\nCheck your calorie target again here: {{link_kit}}\nThe full article: {{link_article:calorie-deficit-how-much-to-eat}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Written coaching, if you want it", preheader: "A 3 minute report. A written plan back. No calls.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nKeep about 80% of your food as a solid base, and leave about 10 to 20% of calories for meals you actually like. One dessert is part of the plan. A party, a restaurant, a visit: they fit if the day still hits calories and protein.\n\nSome people run that alone. If you want me to set the week with you, this is the written version for English-speaking clients.\n\nYou apply in writing. I usually reply within 24 hours on working days, with a few questions. After payment you fill in an intake. Within 48 hours you've calories, protein and macros, a meal plan built around the way you already eat (restaurants included) and a training plan for your real schedule.\n\nThen once a week you send a 3 minute report from your phone: weight, waist, food, steps, how the week went. I answer in writing with the plan for the next week and I recalculate the numbers.\n\nNo calls and no video calls. You get every plan and every number in writing, and you can read it again on the plane.\n\nI take a limited number of clients so I can read every report properly.\n\n{{price_block}}\n\nApply here: {{link_apply}}\n\nOr reply to this email with a few lines: age, job, how a normal week looks, what you already tried. I'll tell you honestly if it makes sense.\n\nIf you're pregnant or breastfeeding, have an eating disorder, take medication that affects weight, or have a medical condition, talk to your doctor first. I'm a coach, not a doctor. This is also not for anyone under 18.\n\nWant more support? Message me for VIP options.\n\nThe 80/20 idea, in full: {{link_article:flexible-dieting}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Protein: 80 kg x 1.6 = 128 g", preheader: "1.6 to 2.2 g per kg. One protein food in every meal.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nIf I could check one thing in your food, it would be protein.\n\nFor most active people, 1.6 to 2.2 g per kg of body weight a day works well. At 80 kg:\n\n80 x 1.6 = 128 g\n80 x 2.2 = 176 g\n\nThat's about 130 to 175 g a day. If you're losing fat, aim at the higher end. You stay fuller, and hunger is lower.\n\nA 2018 meta-analysis of strength training studies found that the benefit of extra protein for muscle flattens out at about 1.6 g per kg a day. I still use 1.6 to 2.2 g, with some margin on top. A 2012 meta-analysis of diet trials found that higher-protein diets gave a bit more fat loss and kept more lean mass than standard-protein diets.\n\nYou don't have to weigh every gram to get close. Put a protein food on the plate at breakfast, lunch and dinner.\n\nMeat and fish. Eggs. Greek yogurt, skyr, quark, cheese. Beans, lentils, tofu. Protein powder on the day normal food doesn't reach the number.\n\nIf you've kidney disease, or any other medical condition, talk to your doctor before you change how much protein you eat. I'm a coach, not a doctor.\n\nThe full guide: {{link_article:how-much-protein}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Pick the weekend meals on Friday morning", preheader: "Write down 1 or 2 meals before Friday night decides for you.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nMonday to Thursday runs on a plan. Friday night is the part nobody wrote down. By Sunday a lot of people have already decided to start again on Monday.\n\nWeekends are a common reason fat loss stalls. People eat more on Saturday and Sunday than on weekdays, and the weekend slows weight loss down or stops it.\n\nTwo findings are easy to skip. The stricter the food rules, the more overeating shows up, and the results get worse. People who tie food to guilt lose weight worse than people who can enjoy the meal and move on. Guilt starts the next round.\n\nFriday morning, take 5 to 10 minutes. Pick 1 or 2 meals you actually want this weekend and write them down. Then tiredness at the table has less room to add a third meal you never chose.\n\nAt the bigger meal, start with protein and vegetables. A solid portion is about 100 to 150 g of meat, fish or quark (a fresh cheese, similar to strained Greek yogurt), plus vegetables. Protein is one of the most filling parts of the meal, so the rest of the plate matters less.\n\nA planned bigger meal has a few marks: you chose it ahead of time, protein and vegetables are on the plate, and the next morning is a normal day. An unplanned one looks different. Nobody chose it, it goes on until you feel sick, and Monday becomes the restart.\n\nIf you went over, Monday is a normal day, without starving, detox or long cardio as a penalty. You can eat 100 to 200 kcal less for two or three days.\n\nIf you overeat again and again until you feel sick, you hide it, or you lose control around food and it scares you, talk to your doctor. An email can't help you with that.\n\nMore on the weekend: {{link_article:weekend-overeating}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "A few drinks, and the extra food", preheader: "About +82 kcal more food after drinking, on average. The late meal costs more.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nBeer with dinner, a glass of wine, a shot on Friday. The weight can still go down.\n\nFat loss still comes down to the calorie deficit for the week. Large reviews don't find a link between light or moderate drinking and weight gain. A glass with dinner a few times in the week is one situation. Six beers on Friday is a different one.\n\nAlcohol raises appetite, and you get less careful about the food in front of you. People eat more afterwards, and they don't eat less later to make up for it. A meta-analysis put that extra food at +343 kJ on average, about +82 kcal. The late pizza or the kebab usually costs more than the drink.\n\nTwo more effects when drinking is a habit, not one glass:\n\nSleep gets worse. Alcohol suppresses REM sleep and breaks the night into pieces, mostly in the second half.\n\nMuscle building gets slower when the dose is high. In one study, muscle protein building after training was 24% lower with alcohol, even with protein in the meal. The dose was about 12 drinks. That's a drunk night, not wine with dinner.\n\nHealth is a separate question from the scale. In 2023 the WHO said that no amount of alcohol is safe for health. Alcohol is a Group 1 carcinogen, the highest category, and it's addictive. A calorie deficit doesn't cancel that.\n\nIf you drink, count the drinks in the day. A few drinks in the week, counted, aren't what stops fat loss.\n\nThe research, in full: {{link_article:alcohol-and-fat-loss}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Sit-ups for 6 weeks. The waist stayed.", preheader: "The muscles got stronger. Abdominal fat didn't change.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\n24 adults trained their abs 5 days a week for 6 weeks, and they kept the same calories. The muscles got stronger. Abdominal fat didn't change in a significant way. The waist didn't either (Vispute, 2011).\n\nThe belly gets smaller when fat goes down on the whole body. For many people the belly is the last place to lose fat.\n\nA realistic pace is about 0.3 to 0.7 kg of fat a week.\n\n2 x 0.3 to 0.7 kg = 0.6 to 1.4 kg of fat in 14 days.\n\nYou will notice that on the scale. It isn't a finished belly. A promise of a flat belly in 14 days is counting on water loss, and that water comes back after a few days.\n\nMeasure the waist once a week, the same way each time, next to the weekly average on the scale. In the sit-up study the waist stayed put because the calories stayed the same.\n\nWhat helps the fat go: a mild deficit you can keep for months, protein around 1.6 to 2.2 g per kg, and lifting 2 to 4 times a week so the loss is fat and the muscle stays. Walking raises daily energy use without wearing you out. Too little sleep and constant stress can slow the loss and add to fat around the belly. They also make a deficit harder to hold.\n\nIf you suspect a hormone problem, or you take medication, talk to your doctor. Don't try to sort that out from an email. I'm a coach, not a doctor.\n\nMore: {{link_article:how-to-lose-belly-fat}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Send me the menu before the dinner", preheader: "Lighter beer 0.5 l is about 185 kcal. I can mark the order in writing.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nBefore you leave for dinner, look up the kcal in one serving of the drink you actually order.\n\nA lighter beer (about 4% alcohol), 0.5 l, is about 185 kcal. A stronger one (about 5%), 0.5 l, is about 237 kcal. A 5 cl vodka shot (50 ml) is about 110 kcal. Dry white wine, 2 dl (200 ml), is about 130 kcal. Brands differ, so check yours. Sweet drinks add sugar on top of the alcohol.\n\nOn the night, stick to one kind of drink, and drink after you've eaten. If you want a record, take a photo of the plate and the glass. Write it down the next day from the photos. That's easier than opening calorie tables in front of friends.\n\nPart of a hangover is dehydration. Alcohol dries you out. Drink water through the evening, and put a full bottle next to the bed.\n\nIn the written coaching you send me the menu on WhatsApp, Monday to Friday: a business dinner, a hotel breakfast, a week of flights. I answer in writing, usually within 24 hours, and I mark what to take. The plan for eating out and travel is built into the week.\n\nNo calls and no video calls. You write when it suits you. I'm in Czechia. Nothing in this needs us online at the same time.\n\n{{price_block}}\n\nApply here: {{link_apply}}\n\nWant more support? Message me for VIP options.\n\nDrink numbers, in full: {{link_article:drinks-dinners-and-social-events}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "The scale is flat for two weeks", preheader: "Change one thing. About 10% less food, or 2,000 extra steps. Not both.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nTwo weeks on the same plan, and the weekly average on the scale hasn't moved.\n\nOne week is too short to judge. Water, food still in the gut, and for women the phase of the cycle can move the scale by two kilos inside that week. The swing says nothing about fat.\n\nWeigh every morning after the toilet, and compare weekly averages. If the average stays flat for two weeks in a row, and the log is honest, change one thing:\n\ncut about 10% of calories, or add 2,000 steps a day.\n\nOne of the two. Not both in the same week.\n\nSkip the extra bans. Throwing out bread and cutting fruit won't help when the total intake doesn't fit.\n\nThe first four weeks, and this rule: {{link_article:how-to-start-losing-weight}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "1,500 kcal of walking. Almost 40% came back.", preheader: "A small study, 16 people, 12 weeks. Food still sets fat loss.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nA walking plan burned about 1,500 kcal extra per week. The people kept it up for 12 weeks. On average the body took almost 40% of that back. Resting energy use dropped, and so did the energy burned during sleep. Walking got more efficient, and through the day they moved a little less without noticing.\n\nThe study was small. 16 sedentary adults with overweight. Results varied a lot between people (Knaan and colleagues, Communications Medicine, 2026). Use it as a reason to stop expecting steps to do the whole job.\n\nSeparate from that study: people often underreport what they eat, in some cases by 20 to 50%. Oil poured by eye. A piece of chocolate. A glass of wine. The rest of a child's plate. For your health, walk anyway. For fat loss, the food number has to be real.\n\nThat's the part I do with clients in writing. Every week you send the 3 minute report, and I recalculate calories and macros from it. The number moves with the week. You aren't stuck with the first guess from the calculator.\n\nNo calls and no video calls.\n\n{{price_block}}\n\nApply here: {{link_apply}}\n\nWant more support? Message me for VIP options.\n\nThe walking study: {{link_article:10000-steps-and-no-weight-loss}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "One hour of lifting in a bad week", preheader: "Two times 30 minutes. The big jump was between zero and some.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nSixty minutes in the whole week. That's two workouts of 30 minutes, or three of 20.\n\nThe number comes from a 2022 meta-analysis on strength training and risk of death (Shailendra and colleagues). Any lifting, compared with none, was linked to a 15% lower risk of death from all causes. In the studies that could show dose, the lowest risk was about 27% lower, around 60 minutes a week. Above that, the advantage got smaller.\n\nThis is observational. It shows a link, and it isn't proof that lifting causes a longer life. People who lift also sleep more, smoke less, and have different access to doctors. The papers are about risk of death, not about kilos of fat.\n\nFor a week that breaks, the useful part is the size. One hour fits. And the biggest difference in that research was between no lifting and some lifting.\n\nA simple hour:\n\nTwo full-body workouts, 30 minutes each. Four or five exercises: something for the legs (a squat or a lunge), a pull, a push, and one for the core. Two or three sets of 6 to 12 reps. The last two reps should feel heavy. You don't have to force the last possible rep. Write down the weight and the reps. When every set reaches the top of the range, add a little weight next time.\n\nAt home, body weight and a band are enough at the start. A gym helps later, when you need more load.\n\nIf you've had surgery, heart problems, or back pain that limits you, talk to your doctor or a physiotherapist before you start.\n\nMore: {{link_article:strength-training-one-hour-a-week}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "When the same menu stops working", preheader: "The shift is small. WhatsApp Monday to Friday, usually within 24 hours.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nYou've held the same menu for a long time, and now the weight just sits there.\n\nIf the diet has been long, the body does adapt. The name for it's adaptive thermogenesis. Resting energy use goes down a bit, and you move a bit less without noticing. The menu that used to work now holds your weight.\n\nThe size of that shift matters. It's small, nowhere near half of your energy use. The deficit still works. You may need to fine-tune the number. When you raise intake again, the adaptation fades. Nothing is permanently broken.\n\nThat fine-tune is what the written check-in is for. Once a week you send a 3 minute report from your phone. I answer in writing with the plan for next week, and I move calories and macros from what the week actually did.\n\nBetween reports, WhatsApp Monday to Friday. A menu, a hotel breakfast, a week that broke. I usually reply within 24 hours on working days. You write when it suits you. I'm in Czechia. Nothing needs us online at the same time.\n\nNo calls and no video calls. My English is better on paper than on a call, and I'd rather give you a precise answer than a fast one.\n\n{{price_block}}\n\nApply here: {{link_apply}}\n\nWant more support? Message me for VIP options.\n\nIf something feels seriously off (marked fatigue, feeling cold, hair loss, dry skin, swelling, a very low mood), don't guess, and don't read forums. See a doctor. I'm a coach, not a doctor.\n\nMore on this: {{link_article:slow-metabolism-myth}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Dinner at 22:30 is still just dinner", preheader: "82 women, 12 weeks. Early dinner lost 6.7 kg, late dinner lost 4.8.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nThe work dinner is at 22:30. You're in a hotel. Someone once told you that nothing after 6 pm goes straight to your waist.\n\nA bread roll at 10 pm isn't fatter than the same roll at 8 am. Your body cares about energy in and out over the day. The clock doesn't decide that. Eat less than you burn across that day and you lose weight. There's no switch at 6 pm that stores the meal as fat.\n\nTiming still matters a little. In a trial of 82 women on the same weight loss plan, dinner at 19:00 to 19:30 led to 6.7 kg lost in 12 weeks. Dinner at 22:30 to 23:00 led to 4.8 kg lost. An earlier dinner can help a little, mostly because the evening snacks shrink. It helps a bit, that's all.\n\nThe trip usually fails the other way. You eat almost nothing all day to pay for the late dinner, then the dinner and the food after it are where the day goes over. Keep a real dinner, even a late one. Put protein and fiber on that plate so the night isn't a second meal an hour later.\n\nFit the hotel dinner into the day total. The day total is what decides.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:eating-late-at-night}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "The fridge after a hard day", preheader: "Sweet food is the fast fix. It doesn't build a belly against a deficit.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nA long day, then the kitchen. The thing you want is sweet, and you want it now.\n\nChronic stress and short sleep can slow fat loss, and they can add to fat stored around the belly. Cortisol, the stress hormone, gets the blame, and people call the result a hormonal belly. But there's a limit. Hormones don't build a belly on their own against a calorie deficit. They make the deficit harder to hold. Sleep and stress change how easily you stay under your number, and how much you eat. They don't get around the number.\n\nA very hard cut pushes the same door from the other side. Heavy tiredness and hunger are the body asking for more food, and spending less energy. Sweet food is the fastest energy, so that's the signal. Beans do the other job: protein and fiber with the carbs, slower digestion, steadier blood sugar.\n\nFor a week like that:\n\nKeep a regular time to sleep and to get up. Fewer screens in the evening. A walk still counts.\n\nPut protein and fiber in the meal before the hour when the cravings usually show up.\n\nIf you're very tired and very hungry, a diet break can help. That means a planned pause from dieting, not a blow-up and a restart on Monday.\n\nIf you suspect a hormone problem, or you take medication, talk to your doctor. I'm a coach, not a doctor.\n\nStress and the belly: {{link_article:how-to-lose-belly-fat}}\nHunger and cravings: {{link_article:cravings-and-hunger}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "21% with lifting. 40% with lifting plus walking.", preheader: "11 studies, 370,256 people. I write the hour into your real week.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nA 2019 review pooled 11 studies and 370,256 people, followed for a mean of just under nine years (Saeidifard and colleagues).\n\nStrength training on its own was linked to a 21% lower risk of death from all causes. Strength training plus aerobic activity was linked to a 40% lower risk. About double. The papers say aerobic activity, not a step target. The same article's practical line is that weights and walking add up, so walking is the aerobic part I'd keep.\n\nThis is observational. It shows a link. It doesn't prove the gym caused the difference. Another review doesn't agree on the cancer numbers, so I'd not treat 21 or 40 as an exact promise. The direction is the part I use. Lifting and walking add up. The lifting doesn't have to be five days in the gym.\n\nThese papers count deaths, not centimeters off a waist. They are still why a crowded week doesn't need a new sport. About one hour of lifting, plus the steps you already collect. An earlier email covered how to split that hour when the week breaks. This one is about keeping both: the hour and the walking.\n\nIf you want that hour written onto the days you actually have, that's the coaching. I set the training for your real schedule, in writing. Once a week you send a 3 minute report from your phone, training included. I answer in writing with the next week.\n\nNo calls and no video calls.\n\n{{price_block}}\n\nApply here: {{link_apply}}\n\nWant more support? Message me for VIP options.\n\nThe review: {{link_article:strength-training-one-hour-a-week}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "8.5 hours vs 5.5. Same 3 kg, different split.", preheader: "10 people, 14 days in a lab. Short sleep took more muscle than fat.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nSame diet, 14 days, 10 people in a lab. The scale dropped about 3 kg both times. The split didn't.\n\nWith 8.5 hours in bed they lost 1.4 kg of fat and 1.5 kg of lean mass. With 5.5 hours in bed they lost 0.6 kg of fat and 2.4 kg of lean mass. Short sleep, and most of what left wasn't fat. It was a small study. Everything in it was controlled.\n\nOther work points the same way. I won't add numbers the papers didn't print. People with overweight who slept longer ate less. One night without sleep lowered how much muscle protein the body built. In an 8-week diet, about an hour less sleep on five nights a week, less of the weight lost came from fat.\n\nOutside a lab you also move less when you're tired, and the food log usually runs low. The gap is probably bigger.\n\nI put sleep in the top four, with the deficit, protein, and strength training. One bad night won't wreck the diet. If you already sleep 7 to 9 hours most nights, work on something else. The people who truly do well on very little sleep are rare. We're talking about single families. You're almost certainly not one of them.\n\nThe study: {{link_article:sleep-and-fat-loss}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "10,000 steps was a pedometer slogan", preheader: "Add 2,000 to 3,000 to your own average. Most of the benefit shows up earlier.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nThe number 10,000 didn't come from a fat loss study. In Japan in the 1960s it was a marketing line for a pedometer. The device was called manpo-kei, a \"10,000 steps meter\". A round number that sold.\n\nWhat the health research shows is earlier than that. In a pooled analysis of 15 studies, the drop in the risk of dying leveled off around 6,000 to 8,000 steps for people aged 60 and over, and around 8,000 to 10,000 for younger adults (Paluch, 2022). In about 16,700 older women, about 4,400 steps a day already meant a lower risk than about 2,700, and the benefit leveled off around 7,500 (Lee, 2019). Both papers are about dying, not about kilos of fat. Miss 10,000 and you haven't missed the point.\n\nFor fat loss, start from your own average, not from a stranger's target. Add 2,000 to 3,000 steps. If you walk 4,000 now, 6,000 to 7,000 is the next step. A long-term guide for most active people is 7,000 to 10,000 a day. Use it as a guide.\n\n7,000 steps every day for a year beats 20,000 once a week and a chair the other days.\n\nYou don't need a free hour. Take a call while you walk. Get off one stop early. Use the stairs. The steps sit in the gaps you already have.\n\nOne workout is one hour out of twenty-four. The rest of the day's movement, walking included, often burns more than that hour. Hard intervals also raise hunger. A walk usually doesn't, so you're less likely to eat the session back.\n\nFood still sets the rest of the deficit. Steps are the part you can repeat.\n\nMore: {{link_article:how-many-steps-a-day}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "2,500 kcal extra = 5 days of deficit gone", preheader: "On a trip, one meal can be the exception. Not every dinner.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\n7 days at -500 kcal = -3,500 kcal in the bank.\n\nThen a day with no brakes. 2,000 to 3,000 kcal extra is easy. Take the middle:\n\n2,500 / 500 = 5\n\nFive days of the deficit are gone in one afternoon.\n\nA work trip is where this happens without anyone calling it a cheat day. Breakfast at the hotel, lunch with the team, dinner with the client, and a drink after. Four exceptions is a day off the plan, repeated.\n\nOne relaxed meal fits. A whole day doesn't. Pick the one meal before you fly. Pizza, the tasting menu, whatever you actually want. The next meal is a normal meal. One dinner isn't a free pass for the rest of the city.\n\nIf the foods you like are already in the normal week, you don't need a special day at all.\n\nIn the written coaching I set that before the trip: which meal is the one, and what the other meals look like. You aren't deciding it in the taxi. It's in the week, in writing.\n\nNo calls and no video calls.\n\n{{price_block}}\n\nApply here: {{link_apply}}\n\nWant more support? Message me for VIP options.\n\nThe maths: {{link_article:cheat-day-or-cheat-meal}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Protein per 100 kcal, from the label", preheader: "Same column, two lines. 20 g and 100 kcal isn't 10 g and 200 kcal.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nThe front of the pack can say high protein. The table is the part that tells you if that's true for the calories.\n\nRead protein and energy from the same column. Then the serving size drops out of the comparison.\n\nProtein per 100 kcal = protein grams / kcal x 100.\n\nIf that column says 20 g of protein and 100 kcal:\n\n20 / 100 x 100 = 20 g of protein per 100 kcal.\n\nIf it says 10 g of protein and 200 kcal:\n\n10 / 200 x 100 = 5 g per 100 kcal.\n\nThe first line gives you 4 times as much protein for the calories. Those two lines are examples of the arithmetic, not two foods from a shop. Use the numbers printed on the pack in front of you.\n\nThat's the same ranking as the protein tool. It sorts common foods by protein per 100 kcal, so you can see which ones reach your grams without spending the whole day of calories. You can also type the price and the pack size from your own shop and see what 100 g of protein costs you.\n\nhttps://martinbarna.cz/en/protein/\n\nAn earlier email set the daily grams. This one is how to pick the food that buys those grams.\n\nThe article: {{link_article:how-much-protein}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Willpower is rarely why the weight comes back", preheader: "50 people, appetite hormones still off a year later. Change the setup.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\n\"I'll be strict for a month, then eat normally again.\"\n\nLook at the word normally. If normally means the way of eating that put the weight on, you get the same result. A diet with an end date doesn't hold. A change you can still keep in a year does.\n\nYo-yo dieting usually comes from a bad setup. Your character has little to do with it. Three things show up together: a deficit that's too big, muscle lost along the way, and a plan that was always going to stop.\n\nLess muscle means you burn less at rest, including on the couch. Fat then comes back more easily when the old food returns.\n\nThere's a second push, and it isn't in your head. Sumithran and colleagues (2011) measured 50 people. Appetite hormones hadn't returned to normal even a year after the diet. Hunger stays louder. For most people you can't switch that off with willpower overnight.\n\nA different study followed 14 people from an extreme weight-loss competition for six years. Resting metabolism stayed about 700 kcal a day lower. That's an extreme case, not a normal diet. Don't apply 700 kcal to yourself. On a mild deficit the picture I see is different: you want the old portions, and the hunger lasts longer than you hoped. The harder you cut, the harder the body defends the old weight.\n\nThe setup that lasts:\n\na mild deficit, 15 to 20% below your energy use\nprotein around 1.6 to 2 g per kg\nlifting, so the body has a reason to keep muscle\nafter the diet, add calories back to maintenance step by step, and stay there for a while\n\nFat loss is a way of eating you can live with, long after the month is over.\n\nMore: {{link_article:yo-yo-dieting}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "A flat scale at 45 isn't a dead metabolism", preheader: "Energy use per kg of muscle stays stable from 20 to 60. The legs went quiet.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nThe weekly average has sat still, and the first story is age. Forty, forty-five, fifty. The metabolism must have switched off.\n\nThe biggest set of measurements we have says otherwise. Daily energy use, measured with doubly labeled water, covers people from 8 days old to 95 years (Pontzer, 2021). Per kg of fat-free mass, muscle and organs, energy use stays stable from 20 to 60. It starts to fall only later. Age 40 isn't a break point in that data.\n\nWhat does change is the muscle itself. An MRI study of 468 men and women, aged 18 to 88, found the share of muscle in body weight falling from the 20s. A clear drop in the amount of muscle showed up toward the late 40s, mostly in the lower half of the body (Janssen, 2000). Less muscle, less energy use. The cause is often what left the day: stairs, longer walks, the car and the desk. Not a switch thrown on a birthday.\n\nSo a flat month isn't a reason to crash the food to 1,200 kcal. After 40 that trade is worse than at 20. More muscle goes with it, and at 50 muscle comes back more slowly than it left.\n\nAn earlier email already has the small move when two honest weeks are flat. This one is why you don't throw the mild plan out because a birthday arrived. Keep the muscle. Keep the walking. Judge the trend over months, not over two weeks.\n\nMore: {{link_article:fat-loss-after-40}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Protein and lifting worked together", preheader: "0.6 kg of fat-free mass in the group that did both. I set both in writing.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nA Dutch study put 100 people, aged 55 to 80, on a calorie deficit for ten weeks. Four groups: more protein or not, weight training three times a week or not (Verreijen, 2017).\n\nIn the main analysis, protein alone didn't change fat-free mass in a clear way. Training alone didn't either. The only group whose fat-free mass went up during the loss did both, by 0.6 kg (p = 0.011).\n\nRead the small print before you quote 0.6 kg at anyone. The people were older than many readers of that paper. The result sat inside one group. It wasn't the headline effect of the whole study. The direction is what I take from it. The two levers work together. Each one on its own pulls less.\n\nThat's how the written start is built. After payment you fill in an intake. Within 48 hours you've calories, protein and a training plan together, not one of them and a promise about the other. The minimum version for a messy week is agreed before that week arrives. One short guide each week: the thing your last report actually needs.\n\nNo calls and no video calls. You get the plan and the numbers in writing.\n\nIf you take medication that affects weight, talk to your doctor first. I'm a coach, not a doctor.\n\n{{price_block}}\n\nApply here: {{link_apply}}\n\nWant more support? Message me for VIP options.\n\nThe study: {{link_article:fat-loss-after-40}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "A work dinner you didn't choose", preheader: "Alcohol is 7 kcal per gram. Pick the drink before the first toast.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nSomeone else booked the restaurant. The toast is already on the agenda. You can still lose fat. You need the decision before you sit down.\n\nAlcohol has 7 kcal per gram. Protein and carbs have 4 kcal per gram. Fat has 9. The stronger the drink, the more alcohol in the glass, and the more energy. Your body doesn't need it. It's still energy, same as the rest of the day.\n\nBefore the dinner, write one line for the drink you'll actually order: the name, the pour, the kcal. Use that line again at the next event. Brands differ, so check yours once, not from memory at the table.\n\nOn the night, stick to that one kind. Drink it after you've eaten. If you drink, write it down. A couple of lighter drinks, counted, is a different evening from one that ends drunk.\n\nIf you trained that day, a drunk finish is the expensive version. Your liver clears the alcohol first. Muscle recovery waits. Stay below drunk and the load is far smaller. The feeling is the guide. The rest depends on what you care about this month.\n\nNobody needs alcohol for the deficit to work. If you've some, count it, and the week can still go down.\n\nThe numbers and the rules: {{link_article:drinks-dinners-and-social-events}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "The minimum when the hotel has no gym", preheader: "Log the meal when you eat it. From 3,000 steps, aim at 6,000. Not 12,000.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nNo gym this week. The hotel one is closed, or you already know you won't use it. Fat loss doesn't close with it. You lose a lever, so the food has to be more accurate, and you pay for the missing training somewhere else.\n\nLog the meal when you eat it, not at midnight from memory. The misses are the oil, the bites, the drink you didn't write down. Looking back is where the day goes soft.\n\nProtein is the weaker stand-in for the gym, not a replacement. A meta-analysis of 24 studies and 1,063 people compared diets with the same calories and the same fat, and more protein versus less (Wycherley, 2012). Higher protein meant 0.79 kg more weight loss and 0.87 kg more fat loss. It softened the loss of fat-free mass by 0.43 kg, and it softened the drop in resting energy use by 595 kJ a day, about 142 kcal. The authors call that modest. So do I. Think of it as insurance, then put a protein food in the meals you can actually get.\n\nSteps: move from where you're. If you walk 3,000 now, a goal of 6,000 will do more than a fantasy 12,000. Stairs and the walk between meetings count. You don't need a sports session to change that line.\n\nOne warning. A study that showed a large drop from diet alone used about 600 kcal a day for 8 weeks. That's an extreme diet, far from this minimum. Don't copy it in a hotel.\n\nWhen the week is normal again, add the lifting back. Until then, food and steps still move the result.\n\nMore: {{link_article:lose-weight-without-exercise}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "The diet cola at the airport is fine", preheader: "People who swapped sugary drinks for diet ones lost more weight than people who swapped them for water.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nAirport lounge, long afternoon, you want something cold. Plenty of people take the regular cola because they heard the diet one is worse. It ruins your gut, it makes you crave sweets.\n\nThe studies in people say otherwise.\n\nWhen diet sodas replace sugary drinks, people lose weight and their metabolic markers improve. One meta-analysis went a step further. People who switched from sugary drinks to diet drinks ended up drinking less of the sugary kind, and they lost more weight than people who switched to water.\n\nThe gut story hasn't held up in humans either. A randomized, double-blind crossover trial on aspartame found no change in the microbiome. A later review said the same for aspartame and acesulfame-K: they don't usually affect the human microbiome.\n\nSo on the road, if it's regular or diet, take the diet one. Water works too. The sugary drink is where calories leak, because you don't even notice you drank them.\n\nOne condition. This works when the rest of your day has enough protein and vegetables. A diet cola won't rescue a day of pastries. And if you have a diagnosis, ask your doctor.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:diet-soda-and-weight-loss}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "About 14 g of fiber per 1,000 kcal", preheader: "Almost no calories, and you stay full longer. Add it slowly.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nLook at a typical business lunch: meat, a white side, a sauce. The protein is there. The fiber isn't, and that's one reason you're hungry again so soon.\n\nFiber is the part of plant food your body can't digest. It brings almost no calories and it still does a lot. It fills you up, so you go longer without hunger and pick at food less. It slows down how fast sugar gets absorbed, so you get fewer energy swings and fewer sweet cravings.\n\nThe target is about 30 to 38 g a day. Easy rule: about 14 g per 1,000 kcal. On 2,000 kcal a day that's about 28 g.\n\nWhere to find it on a menu or a hotel buffet:\n\n- Vegetables, as a side or a salad, with every meal you can.\n- Pulses: lentils, beans, chickpeas.\n- Oats and whole grains.\n- Fruit, with the skin where that makes sense.\n- Nuts and seeds.\n\nYou don't have to eat like a rabbit. A portion of vegetables at each meal and pulses a few times a week is enough to start.\n\nOne warning: don't go from almost nothing to a lot overnight. You can feel bloated for the first few days. Add it step by step and drink enough water.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:fiber-and-fat-loss}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "12 sets or 30 on a hard diet", preheader: "Both groups kept their lean mass. More sets added more strength. It's still a preprint.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nWhen people start a diet, a lot of them cut their training in half. Less food, worse recovery, so less work. Sounds logical.\n\nA recent study pushes back on that. Advanced lifters ate in a 40% deficit, with plenty of protein, for four weeks. Half trained 12 sets a week per muscle. The other half trained 30.\n\nOn DXA scans there was no statistically significant difference in body composition. Both groups lost fat and kept their lean mass. Strength is where they differed. The 30-set group added 15 to 27% on the strength tests, the 12-set group 7 to 14%.\n\nNow the small print. It's a preprint, still under review. Four weeks and a DXA scan can't tell real muscle growth from the body simply learning the movement. A 40% cut is aggressive, and these people already lifted. For me it's a signal worth watching. I wouldn't build a rule on it yet.\n\nWhat I take from it: don't cut sets on a diet just out of habit. Let the plan decide that.\n\nThat's what the written training plan in coaching is for. I set it for the schedule you actually have. Once a week you send me a 3 minute report from your phone, training included. I answer in writing with the plan for the next week.\n\nNo calls and no video calls.\n\n{{price_block}}\n\nApply here: {{link_apply}}\n\nWant more support? Message me for VIP options.\n\nThe study: {{link_article:training-volume-while-dieting}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Protein after the gym: the day total decides more", preheader: "Strength climbs up to about 1.5 g per kg. Above that, the curve flattens.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nA late session in the hotel gym, then a meeting. People ask me if the shake has to land the minute the last set ends. What decides more is how much protein you eat across the whole day.\n\nOn paper, two numbers look like they disagree. Stuart Phillips says 1.6 g per kg of body weight covers 97 to 98% of people. Refalo and Helms (2025) say 1.9 to 3.2 g per kg. Their range is per kg of fat-free mass, though, not of what the scale shows. Convert it for a typical lifter at 18 to 28% body fat and their lower edge lands near 1.4 to 1.6 g per kg of body weight. At the low end the two meet. The top end is still debated.\n\nOn strength, Tagawa (2022) shows the curve climbing up to about 1.5 g per kg. Above that it flattens. More protein stops buying more strength in a straight line.\n\nWhat I use with clients: 1.6 to 2.2 g per kg of total body weight. I go toward 2.2 as insurance when the food log is rough (the error is often 20 to 50%) or the fat-loss phase is hard.\n\nAnd protein isn't the first lever. Training, calories and sleep come first. Without those, protein is an expensive insurance policy.\n\nSo on a trip, don't chase the shake. Get protein into the meals you actually get that day.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:protein-timing-after-workout}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "A planned week at maintenance isn't quitting", preheader: "One study liked diet breaks a lot. Studies from 2021 didn't. The pause still has a use.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nA conference week, or the family visiting. In weeks like that I usually hear about two options: push the diet through, or drop everything. There's a third one. A planned break at maintenance.\n\nOne study compared two groups. Group 1 dieted for 16 weeks at 67% of their usual calories. Group 2 also dieted for 16 weeks, but after every two weeks of diet they ate at maintenance for two weeks, so their whole diet took 30 weeks. Group 2 lost almost twice the fat over that period, kept more muscle, and regained much less fat afterwards.\n\nThen came studies in 2021. They found no advantage for breaks over steady fat loss. So I don't sell a break as a fat-loss trick.\n\nIt still earns its place. A hard week, a party, a stretch where you need rest and don't want to gain. Eat at your current maintenance and you don't put fat back on. You just pause the loss.\n\nOne catch. Maintenance drops as your weight drops. If you held your weight on 2,000 kcal at the start, a few weeks later that number is lower. The break week isn't the menu from before the diet.\n\nChanging your body takes months. You don't have to lose weight every single week.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:diet-breaks}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Night shifts make fat loss harder, not impossible", preheader: "About 23% higher obesity risk in shift workers. The physics still holds.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nThis one is for you if you work nights or a rotating roster. If that's not you, feel free to skip it.\n\nShift work has a measurable effect. A 2018 meta-analysis put obesity risk about 23% higher in shift workers. A 2021 meta-analysis put the risk of metabolic syndrome about 30% higher. It's not your weak will.\n\nWhat happens: a knocked-about body clock scrambles hunger and fullness. Your body isn't sure when it has had enough. Add worse sleep, a pull toward something fast, and whatever food is on offer at 3 a.m.\n\nThe physics still holds on a night shift. Calories don't disappear. What shifts break is your ability to stick with the plan. So the setup has to count on a day that doesn't look like a textbook.\n\n- Put the main meal before the shift or after it, not in the middle of the night.\n- Bring food from home, so 3 a.m. doesn't choose for you.\n- Make sleep the priority. Don't answer a bad week by cutting calories harder. Less sleep means more hunger.\n- A planned nap of 20 to 30 minutes in the middle of the shift helps energy and cravings.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:shift-work-and-fat-loss}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Decide the default before you're hungry", preheader: "A plan you never touch goes stale. A plan you edit every week keeps working.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nOn a busy day, food decisions happen at the worst moment. You're hungry, you're late, and whatever is closest wins.\n\nPlanning moves the decision earlier. When the meals are decided ahead of time, you know what the day holds, and the random snack you grab in a rush is easier to skip. Same on a trip: decide the default before you're hungry.\n\nThe deficit doesn't need to be dramatic. 300 to 500 kcal below what your day burns is a deficit you can actually keep. A steady rhythm helps, for example 3 main meals and 1 or 2 snacks. The number of meals won't speed up your metabolism. It only has to fit your calendar.\n\nThe part people skip is the edit. A plan you never touch goes stale. A plan you look at again each week keeps working.\n\nThat weekly edit is the core of written coaching. Once a week you send me a 3 minute report from your phone. I answer in writing with the plan for next week, built on what the last week actually did. The minimum version for a messy week is agreed before that week arrives, so you're not making it up in the middle of it.\n\nNo calls and no video calls.\n\n{{price_block}}\n\nApply here: {{link_apply}}\n\nWant more support? Message me for VIP options.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:meal-planning-for-fat-loss}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "The hotel breakfast is optional", preheader: "A 2014 trial and a 2019 BMJ meta-analysis found no meaningful effect of breakfast on weight.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nThe hotel breakfast is included and the buffet is open all morning. A lot of people eat it because it's paid for, and because breakfast is \"the most important meal of the day\".\n\nThat line comes from advertising.\n\nThe trials: in 2014, a group around David Allison randomly told people to eat breakfast or skip it. No clinically meaningful difference in weight. In 2019, a BMJ meta-analysis of randomized trials found that people who added breakfast tended to eat a bit more across the day. Breakfast wasn't a reliable weight loss tool.\n\nSkipping it won't slow your metabolism either. The energy you spend digesting food follows how much you eat across the day, whatever time you start.\n\nSo on a trip the question is simple. Does breakfast help you hold the day, or does it get in the way?\n\n- Eat it if you're hungry in the morning, if you train early, or if skipping it makes the evening hunger hit hard.\n- Skip it calmly if you're not hungry and you only eat it because it's there.\n\nOne rule either way. Don't skip breakfast \"for fat loss\" and then make up for it at night. If that happens to you, eat the breakfast, with protein in it.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:do-you-need-breakfast-to-lose-weight}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Low-carb or low-fat? About 16 g a day apart.", preheader: "Set calories and protein. Split carbs and fat by taste and training.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nSome people weigh every gram of carbs and fat, sure that the ratio decides their fat loss. Once calories and protein are set, the ratio barely moves the result.\n\nHall and Guo went through 32 tightly controlled studies that kept protein the same. The difference in fat loss between high-carb and low-carb came out at about 16 g a day. And that small edge went to the lower-fat diets.\n\nSacks followed 811 people for about two years on four different ratios. They lost about the same amount of weight.\n\nProtein is a different story. Leidy found that at the same weight loss, higher protein kept almost half more muscle than lower protein.\n\nSo what to track: calories, protein, and fiber for fullness. Split carbs and fat by taste and by training. That's good news on the road. Pasta or a steak can both fit, as long as the day's total and the protein hold.\n\nTwo limits. Keep fat at roughly 25 to 35% of calories and don't live under 20% for long. And carbs fuel hard training.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:macros-and-fat-loss}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "48 kcal a minute vs 31", preheader: "Same calories on offer. People ate 508 kcal a day more on the ultra-processed menu.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nA bag of salty sticks in the hotel room is gone in ten minutes. A plate of potatoes and chicken isn't. There's a measured reason for that.\n\nKevin Hall kept 20 adults on a ward for a month (2019). Two weeks on ultra-processed food, two weeks on unprocessed food. Both menus offered matched calories, macros, sugar, salt and fiber, and people could eat as much as they wanted. On the ultra-processed menu they ate 508 kcal a day more and gained 0.9 kg in those two weeks. On the unprocessed menu they lost 0.9 kg.\n\nThe physics worked as usual. People gained because they ate more, and the food let them eat more without noticing.\n\nThe lever is speed. A co-author, Ciaran Forde, looked at how fast people ate in that trial. Ultra-processed: 48 kcal a minute. Unprocessed: 31. The fullness signal arrives tens of minutes late. Eat the same energy in half the time and \"I've had enough\" shows up when you're already a few hundred calories ahead.\n\nTexture matters more than the label. In another trial, harder versions of the same lunches were eaten more slowly, and people took in 26% less energy from them.\n\nWhat you can do with any menu or minibar, without banning anything:\n\n- Pick the harder, bulkier version of the same food. Potatoes instead of mash. Nuts instead of a bar.\n- Put the soft, dense food on a plate. Don't eat it from the bag.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:ultra-processed-food}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "A spoon of oil beats a sugar cube", preheader: "Your body counts the calories in the granola, whatever the label says.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nOrganic, fit, raw, superfood. Labels like that make people feel they can eat as much as they want. Your body counts the calories either way.\n\nSome \"healthy\" foods are dense:\n\n- Nuts and nut butters. A good food, and a handful is fine. Half a jar of peanut butter a day can run past a few hundred extra kcal.\n- Oil, including the best olive oil. A spoon of oil has more calories than a sugar cube.\n- Avocado. Half at breakfast is fine. Three a day will show up.\n- Granola, muesli bars, \"fit\" desserts. They often have as much sugar and fat as a regular sweet, with a nicer wrapper and a higher price.\n\nIt works the other way too. A sane amount of chocolate won't make you gain. No food is clean or dirty on its own. Amount and context decide.\n\nThis is how a careful eater can stall: the food is good, the amounts aren't counted. The weekly report in written coaching shows it. Once a week you send me a 3 minute report from your phone. I look at the total for the week and answer in writing with the plan for next week. Calories and macros move based on what the week actually did.\n\nNo calls and no video calls.\n\n{{price_block}}\n\nApply here: {{link_apply}}\n\nWant more support? Message me for VIP options.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:clean-eating-myth}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Eat the fruit. Count the airport smoothie.", preheader: "Two or three portions of whole fruit a day fit a diet. Juice and dried fruit are where it leaks.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\n\"A banana is all sugar.\" \"Fruit makes you fat, it has fructose.\" I hear this a lot, and people end up throwing out one of the best foods they could eat.\n\nFruit contains sugar. Fat gain comes from eating more energy than you spend, for long enough, wherever that energy comes from.\n\nWhole fruit is mostly water and fiber. A big volume brings few calories. An apple or a bowl of berries fills you up before you could overshoot. The fructose worry is about large amounts of liquid, concentrated sugar. A liter of soda, not an apple or a handful of raspberries.\n\nFruit gets away from you in two forms:\n\n- Juice and smoothies. Once the fruit is blended or pressed, much of what made it filling is gone. A few pieces you'd chew for a while go down in half a minute.\n- Dried fruit. The water is gone, so a small handful holds a lot of calories and sugar, and fills you less.\n\nSo on a trip: the fruit bowl at reception, yes. The big airport smoothie is a drink with calories. Count it that way.\n\nWhat I use: two or three portions of whole fruit a day, counted inside the day's calories like everything else. Fruit counts like any other food, and one banana won't wreck a diet.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:fruit-and-weight-loss}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" }
];
